      "summaries": {
        "en": "Newton is an open-source GPU-accelerated physics simulation engine built on NVIDIA Warp that enables realistic robotic and physics simulations. Its key innovation is leveraging GPU parallelization through Warp to achieve real-time performance for complex multi-body dynamics, making high-fidelity simulation accessible. Researchers can use it to prototype robotic controllers, test manipulation tasks, or develop new simulation algorithms by extending its modular Python API for custom physics scenarios.",
        "zh": "Newton是一个基于NVIDIA Warp构建的开源GPU加速物理仿真引擎，专为机器人学和仿真研究设计。它通过Warp实现GPU并行化，能够实时处理复杂的多体动力学问题，让高保真仿真变得高效可行。该引擎采用模块化Python接口，研究人员可快速搭建机器人控制原型、测试抓取任务，或扩展自定义物理场景来开发新算法，为实验提供灵活可靠的仿真环境。",
        "es": "Newton is an open-source GPU-accelerated physics simulation engine built on NVIDIA Warp that enables realistic robotic and physics simulations. Its key innovation is leveraging GPU parallelization through Warp to achieve real-time performance for complex multi-body dynamics, making high-fidelity simulation accessible. Researchers can use it to prototype robotic controllers, test manipulation tasks, or develop new simulation algorithms by extending its modular Python API for custom physics scenarios.",
        "sections": {
          "why": "newton stands out for newton-physics, nvidia-warp, and physics-simulation in real-world workflows.",
          "what": "Newton is an open-source GPU-accelerated physics simulation engine built on NVIDIA Warp that enables realistic robotic and physics simulations. Its key innovation is leveraging GPU parallelization through Warp to achieve real-time performance for complex multi-body dynamics, making high-fidelity simulation accessible. Researchers can use it to prototype robotic controllers, test manipulation tasks, or develop new simulation algorithms by extending its modular Python API for custom physics scenarios.",
          "how": "Review https://github.com/newton-physics/newton for docs, run starter examples, and integrate with your pipeline or evaluation stack."
